# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EVERY VITE_ VARIABLE IS COMPILED INTO THE PUBLIC BUNDLE.
# Treat this file as if it were served to the browser, because it effectively is.
# NEVER put RP_SIGNING_KEY here — that key is the app's identity and belongs in
# Secrets Manager, read only by the Lambda. See backend/README.md.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# World ID app id from https://developer.world.org — starts with `app_`.
#
# This is NOT the rp_id. Both come from the same Developer Portal app:
#   app_id  → identifies the app to World App (public, goes in the client)
#   rp_id   → identifies the relying party when verifying (rp_d8319d06a1241d73)
#
# It must belong to the app that owns rp_d8319d06a1241d73, since that is the
# rp_id the router was deployed with and the backend signs for.
#
# The environment is NOT configured here — it follows the router, and the deployment
# now has one router per environment with a toggle in the page header. Your app id
# must be valid for whichever environment you are testing:
#   production -> real Orb-verified World App
#   staging    -> the simulator
VITE_WORLD_APP_ID=app_

# RPC override. Defaults to the `rpcUrl` recorded in deployments/demo.json by the
# deploy script — localhost for the fork, World Chain for a mainnet deployment — so
# you normally do not need this at all.
#
# Worth setting for a live demo: the depth panel issues one quote per band on every
# amount change, and a public endpoint is the most likely thing to rate-limit at the
# worst moment. A dedicated provider (Alchemy, QuickNode, drpc paid) fixes that.
#
# ⚠ This value is COMPILED INTO THE PUBLIC BUNDLE, so a provider key placed here is
# public. Restrict it by referrer/domain in the provider dashboard before using it,
# or proxy through /api instead. An unrestricted key in here is someone else's free
# RPC quota.
# VITE_RPC_URL=https://worldchain-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<domain-restricted-key>

# ── Diagnostics for "Verification unavailable" ───────────────────────────────
# environment normally derives from the router's verifier (staging here). Set
# this only to test whether a staging/production mismatch is the cause.
# VITE_WORLD_ENV=production
#
# require_user_presence defaults on for production and is never sent for staging —
# the simulator has no camera, and asking it for a liveness check makes it refuse the
# whole request with an opaque "Something went wrong". Set this to false to drop the
# request on production too, when bisecting a failing production request.
# VITE_REQUIRE_PRESENCE=false
